# DESCRIPTION
# A scaffolded problem where students use one limit law or operation at a time to compute a limit.
# ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject(Calculus - single variable)
## DBchapter(Limits and continuity)
## DBsection(Rules of limits - basic)
## KEYWORDS('calculus','limits','Limit')
## Level(2)
## MO(1)
## Language(en)
## TitleText1('Calculus')
## EditionText1('6')
## AuthorText1('Stewart')
## Section1('2.3')
## Problem1('')
## Author('Chenfei Peng, Mercredi Chasman')
## Institution('UMinnMorris, 12/13/2024')

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros(
    "PGstandard.pl", "PGML.pl", 'scaffold.pl', 'parserPopUp.pl',
    "PGcourse.pl",
);

@laws = (
    'Sum/difference law',
    'Constant Multiple Law',
    'Constant Law',
    'Power Law',
    'Quotient Law',
    'Product Law',
    'Linear Law'
);

$popup1 = DropDown([@laws], 'Sum/difference law');
$popup2 = DropDown([@laws], 'Constant Multiple Law');
$popup3 = DropDown([@laws], 'Power Law');
$popup4 = DropDown([@laws], 'Linear Law');

Context("Numeric");

BEGIN_PGML
Use limit laws to compute the following limit. Use only one rule at a time. Answer blanks should be filled with mathematical expressions or numbers as appropriate.
END_PGML

Scaffold::Begin(
    can_open => "when_previous_correct",
    is_open  => "correct_or_first_incorrect"
);

Section::Begin('Step 1');

$a0       = random(-1, 1, 2) * random(2, 7);
$b0       = random( 2, 15);
$oper0    = list_random('+', '-');
$c        = random(-5, 5);
$form1    = Compute("$a0 x^2 $oper0 $b0 x")->reduce;
$operands = [ '\(+\)', '\(-\)', '\(\cdot\)', '\(\div\)', '^' ];

BEGIN_PGML
[``
\lim_{x \to [$c]} \Big[[$a0]x^2 [$oper0] [$b0] x\Big] = \lim_{x \to [$c]}\Big[ ``] [___]{Compute("$a0 x^2")} [``\Big]``]
[___]{DropDown($operands, "\\($oper0\\)", useHTMLSelect => 0)}       [``\lim_{x \to [$c]}\Big[ ``] [_]{Formula("$b0 x")} [``\Big]``]

*Which limit law applies here?*
[________]{$popup1}
END_PGML

Section::End();

Section::Begin('Step 2');
BEGIN_PGML

[``
= ``] [___]{$a0} [`` \lim_{x \to [$c]}\Big[ ``] [___]{Formula("x^2")} [``\Big] [$oper0] ``]  [_]{$b0} [``\lim_{x \to [$c]}\Big[ ``] [_]{Formula('x')} [``\Big]``]

*Which limit law applies here?*
[________]{$popup2}
END_PGML
Section::End();

Section::Begin('Step 3');
BEGIN_PGML

[``
= [$a0] ( \lim_{x \to [$c]} \Big[ ``] [___]{Formula('x')} [``\Big] )^2 [$oper0] [$b0] \lim_{x \to [$c]} \Big[ ``] [_]{Formula('x')} [``\Big]``]

*Which limit law applies here?*
[________]{$popup3}
END_PGML
Section::End();

Section::Begin('Step 4');
BEGIN_PGML

[`` = [$a0] ( ``] [___]{$c} [`` )^2 [$oper0] [$b0] ( ``] [___]{$c} [`` ) ``]

*Which limit law applies here?*
[________]{$popup4}
END_PGML
Section::End();

Section::Begin('Step 5');
BEGIN_PGML

[`` = ``] [___]{$form1->eval(x => $c)}

END_PGML
Section::End();

Scaffold::End();

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
In this solution, we apply the limit laws step by step to simplify the expression and find the value of the limit.

- **Step 1**: Apply the **Sum/Difference Law** to split the limit.
- **Step 2**: Apply the **Constant Multiple Law** to factor out the constants.
- **Step 3**: Use the **Power Law** to deal with the powers of [` x `].
- **Step 4**: Evaluate the limits by substitution.
- **Step 5**: Combine the results to get the final answer.

Final result: [$form1->eval(x => $c)].
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
